                        data-link-purpose-append="in this tab"
                        data-link-purpose-append-open="in this tab">
                    Open all abstracts<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;in this tab</span></button></p><!--    articleEntryList start--><ul class="art-list"><li class="art-list-item reveal-container reveal-closed"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="offscreen-hidden">The following article is </span><span class="red">Open access</span></div><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed" class="art-list-item-title event_main-link">A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs</a><p class="small art-list-item-meta">Jonathan Gagné <em>et al</em> 2026 <em>ApJ</em> <b>1009</b> 15 </p><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-upper"><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed/meta" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="View article"><span class="icon-article"></span>View article<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed/pdf" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="PDF"><span class="icon-file-pdf"></span>PDF<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed/epub" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="ePub"><span class="icon-epub"></span><span class="offscreen-hidden">Download&nbsp;</span>ePub<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs</span></a><button type="button" class="reveal-trigger mr-2 nowrap"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg><span class="reveal-trigger-label" data-reveal-text="Open abstract" data-reveal-label-alt="Close abstract" data-link-purpose-append="A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs" data-link-purpose-append-open="A SPHEREx Pipeline and Spectral Library for Ultracool Dwarfs">Open abstract</span></button></div><div class="reveal-content"><div class="article-text view-text-small"><p>We present a Python spectrophotometry extraction tool tailored for fast-moving point sources detected in the SPHEREx mission and use it to construct a set of 0.75–5.0 <i>μ</i>m low-resolution (<i>λ</i>/Δ<i>λ</i> ≈ 50) spectrophotometry data products based on the SPHEREx Quick Release 2 (QR2) for a set of 6003 L0–Y1 ultracool dwarfs: 2050 known ultracool dwarfs, 3008 known photometric ultracool dwarf candidates, and 945 newly identified ultracool dwarfs. This work more than doubles the number of ultracool dwarfs with spectroscopy, from 3449 to 7402. We provide SPHEREx templates for each spectral subtype and a set of tools to assign automated spectral types. The QR2 data release generates spectrophotometry with an average signal-to-noise per spectral channel above ≈10 for most objects with WISE W2 magnitudes of 14.0 mag and brighter. The compiled data set is made available publicly at <a href="https://mocadb.ca">https://mocadb.ca</a>, where new spectral compilations from future data releases will also be made available as they are published. These new data provide a significant increase in the number of substellar objects for which the 2.4–5.0 <i>μ</i>m window is now accessible, making it possible to probe important molecular chemistry of key CNOS-bearing species for the coolest brown dwarfs. We flag 2668 ultracool dwarfs as candidate young brown dwarfs, 250 as candidate subdwarfs, and 865 as possibly otherwise peculiar spectroscopic features of interest for future investigation. The SPIFF library presented here opens the door to efficient confirmation of candidate substellar objects and follow-up studies of population-level atmospheric properties of cold brown dwarfs.</p></div><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-lower"><a class="mr-2" href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed">https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae8aed</a></div></div></li><li class="art-list-item reveal-container reveal-closed"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="offscreen-hidden">The following article is </span><span class="red">Open access</span></div><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7" class="art-list-item-title event_main-link">Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk</a><p class="small art-list-item-meta">Zhi-Qiang Liu <em>et al</em> 2026 <em>ApJ</em> <b>1009</b> 66 </p><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-upper"><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7/meta" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="View article"><span class="icon-article"></span>View article<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7/pdf" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="PDF"><span class="icon-file-pdf"></span>PDF<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7/epub" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="ePub"><span class="icon-epub"></span><span class="offscreen-hidden">Download&nbsp;</span>ePub<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk</span></a><button type="button" class="reveal-trigger mr-2 nowrap"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg><span class="reveal-trigger-label" data-reveal-text="Open abstract" data-reveal-label-alt="Close abstract" data-link-purpose-append="Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk" data-link-purpose-append-open="Rapid Orbital Decay of Supersoft X-Ray Source WX Cen: A Surrounding Circumbinary Disk">Open abstract</span></button></div><div class="reveal-content"><div class="article-text view-text-small"><p>WX Cen is most likely a candidate of compact binary supersoft X-ray sources (SSSs), which consists of a white dwarf (WD) and a donor star that fills the Roche lobe. Recently, this source was detected to be experiencing a rapid orbital decay at a rate of <span xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" class="inline-eqn"><span class="tex"><span class="texImage"><img src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNkYAAAAAYAAjCB0C8AAAAASUVORK5CYII=" data-src="https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/1009/1/66/revision1/apjae93a7ieqn1.gif" style="max-width: 100%;" alt="$\dot{P}=-(4.4\pm 0.4)\times 1{0}^{-7}\,{\rm{days}}\,{{\rm{yr}}}^{-1}$" align="top" role="math"></img></span><script type="math/tex">\dot{P}=-(4.4\pm 0.4)\times 1{0}^{-7}\,{\rm{days}}\,{{\rm{yr}}}^{-1}</script></span></span>. According to the mass function and optical eclipses, the donor-star mass can be constrained to be 0.41−0.44, 0.47−0.50, and 0.55−0.59 <i>M</i><sub>⊙</sub> when the WD mass is 0.7, 0.9, and 1.2 <i>M</i><sub>⊙</sub>, respectively. The observed orbital period derivative cannot be produced by the angular momentum loss (AML) due to mass loss during the accretion of the WD and magnetic braking (MB) mechanisms, including standard MB, convection and rotation-enhanced MB, and anomalous MB prescriptions. We propose that the rapid orbital decay of WX Cen is caused by the tidal torque that originates from the resonant interaction between the binary and a surrounding circumbinary (CB) disk. Detailed stellar evolution models indicate that a WD binary with a 2.5 × 10<sup>−7</sup><i>M</i><sub>⊙</sub> CB disk can evolve toward a WX Cen-like system, which has an orbital period derivative of <span xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" class="inline-eqn"><span class="tex"><span class="texImage"><img src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNkYAAAAAYAAjCB0C8AAAAASUVORK5CYII=" data-src="https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/1009/1/66/revision1/apjae93a7ieqn2.gif" style="max-width: 100%;" alt="$\dot{P}=-4.0\times 1{0}^{-7}\,{\rm{days}}\,{{\rm{yr}}}^{-1}$" align="top" role="math"></img></span><script type="math/tex">\dot{P}=-4.0\times 1{0}^{-7}\,{\rm{days}}\,{{\rm{yr}}}^{-1}</script></span></span> and a relatively high mass-transfer rate of 5.3 × 10<sup>−7</sup><i>M</i><sub>⊙</sub> yr<sup>−1</sup> that can trigger a stable hydrogen burning process on the surface of the WD.</p></div><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-lower"><a class="mr-2" href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7">https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93a7</a></div></div></li><li class="art-list-item reveal-container reveal-closed"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="offscreen-hidden">The following article is </span><span class="red">Open access</span></div><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3" class="art-list-item-title event_main-link">Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars</a><p class="small art-list-item-meta">Franco Cabral <em>et al</em> 2026 <em>ApJ</em> <b>1009</b> 65 </p><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-upper"><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3/meta" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="View article"><span class="icon-article"></span>View article<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3/pdf" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="PDF"><span class="icon-file-pdf"></span>PDF<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3/epub" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="ePub"><span class="icon-epub"></span><span class="offscreen-hidden">Download&nbsp;</span>ePub<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars</span></a><button type="button" class="reveal-trigger mr-2 nowrap"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg><span class="reveal-trigger-label" data-reveal-text="Open abstract" data-reveal-label-alt="Close abstract" data-link-purpose-append="Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars" data-link-purpose-append-open="Generalized Predictions for the Electromagnetic Signatures of Mirror Stars">Open abstract</span></button></div><div class="reveal-content"><div class="article-text view-text-small"><p>Mirror stars are a generic prediction of dissipative dark matter (DM) models, including minimal atomic DM and twin baryons in the mirror twin Higgs. Mirror stars capture regular atoms from the interstellar medium through highly suppressed kinetic mixing interactions between the regular and the dark photon. This results in the accumulation of a “nugget,” which draws heat from the mirror star core and emits distinctive X-ray and optical signals. In this work, we solve the stellar structure equations of optically thick nuggets across a wide range of the effective mirror star parameter space, and characterize their emission spectra using stellar atmosphere models. This complements an earlier analysis of lower-mass optically thin nuggets. We find that optically thick mirror star nuggets occupy distinct regions of the (stellar surface temperature, luminosity, surface gravity) space, and can be distinguished from regular stars in both Hertzsprung–Russell diagrams and temperature–surface gravity diagrams using astrometric and spectroscopic stellar catalogues. Our detailed predictions, which are publicly available, now give for the first time a general picture of mirror star signals in the optical and IR to enable realistic mirror star searches using existing catalogues and new telescope observations.</p></div><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-lower"><a class="mr-2" href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3">https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae93b3</a></div></div></li><li class="art-list-item reveal-container reveal-closed"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="offscreen-hidden">The following article is </span><span class="red">Open access</span></div><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478" class="art-list-item-title event_main-link">AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN</a><p class="small art-list-item-meta">Yael Dgany <em>et al</em> 2026 <em>ApJ</em> <b>1009</b> 71 </p><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-upper"><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478/meta" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="View article"><span class="icon-article"></span>View article<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478/pdf" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="PDF"><span class="icon-file-pdf"></span>PDF<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478/epub" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="ePub"><span class="icon-epub"></span><span class="offscreen-hidden">Download&nbsp;</span>ePub<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN</span></a><button type="button" class="reveal-trigger mr-2 nowrap"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg><span class="reveal-trigger-label" data-reveal-text="Open abstract" data-reveal-label-alt="Close abstract" data-link-purpose-append="AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN" data-link-purpose-append-open="AT 2022csn: A Photometrically Peculiar Optical/UV Tidal Disruption Event in a Type II AGN">Open abstract</span></button></div><div class="reveal-content"><div class="article-text view-text-small"><p>The emission mechanism and host galaxy preference of optical/UV tidal disruption events (TDEs) are still not entirely understood. We present observations of the TDE AT 2022csn, which is one of the most distant (<i>d</i><sub><i>L</i></sub> ∼ 726 Mpc) and luminous (<span xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" class="inline-eqn"><span class="tex"><span class="texImage"><img src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNkYAAAAAYAAjCB0C8AAAAASUVORK5CYII=" data-src="https://content.cld.iop.org/journals/0004-637X/1009/1/71/revision1/apjae9478ieqn1.gif" style="max-width: 100%;" alt="${L}_{\mathrm{peak}}=2.48{7}_{-0.067}^{+0.073}\times 1{0}^{44}\,\mathrm{erg}\,{{\rm{s}}}^{-1}$" align="top" role="math"></img></span><script type="math/tex">{L}_{\mathrm{peak}}=2.48{7}_{-0.067}^{+0.073}\times 1{0}^{44}\,\mathrm{erg}\,{{\rm{s}}}^{-1}</script></span></span>) optical/UV TDEs observed to date. Although it is a spectroscopically normal H + He TDE, it shows some photometric peculiarities, exhibiting a pronounced double-peaked light curve (with peaks separated by 18.30 ± 2.84 days in the <i>g </i>band) and lying in the low-temperature and large-radius end of the optical/UV TDE population. The host galaxy of AT 2022csn shows evidence for a significant starburst within the last gigayear, consistent with hosts of other optical/UV TDEs, as well as narrow emission lines, consistent with hosts of Type II AGN. Interaction between the TDE and a preexisting AGN accretion disk might explain the peculiar photometric properties. However, it is puzzling that a TDE would be visible in a Type II AGN, where according to the AGN unification picture the central region around the supermassive black hole is obscured. We suggest a few scenarios to reconcile this. AT 2022csn together with AT 2019ahk, which shows similar properties, may belong to a new subset of low-temperature, high-radius TDEs in galaxies with Type II AGN emission features.</p></div><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-lower"><a class="mr-2" href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478">https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9478</a></div></div></li><li class="art-list-item reveal-container reveal-closed"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="offscreen-hidden">The following article is </span><span class="red">Open access</span></div><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e" class="art-list-item-title event_main-link">X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events</a><p class="small art-list-item-meta">Xiaoshan Huang <em>et al</em> 2026 <em>ApJ</em> <b>1009</b> 64 </p><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-upper"><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e/meta" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="View article"><span class="icon-article"></span>View article<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e/pdf" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="PDF"><span class="icon-file-pdf"></span>PDF<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events</span></a><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e/epub" class="mr-2 mb-0 nowrap event_mini-link" data-event-action="ePub"><span class="icon-epub"></span><span class="offscreen-hidden">Download&nbsp;</span>ePub<span class="offscreen-hidden">,&nbsp;X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events</span></a><button type="button" class="reveal-trigger mr-2 nowrap"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg><span class="reveal-trigger-label" data-reveal-text="Open abstract" data-reveal-label-alt="Close abstract" data-link-purpose-append="X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events" data-link-purpose-append-open="X-Ray Variability and Photosphere Evolution during Accretion Disk Formation in Tidal Disruption Events">Open abstract</span></button></div><div class="reveal-content"><div class="article-text view-text-small"><p>The early time emission in tidal disruption events (TDEs) originates from both accretion and shocks, producing photons that eventually emerge from an inhomogeneous photosphere. We model disk formation following debris stream self-intersection in a TDE using three-dimensional, frequency-integrated, and multigroup radiation hydrodynamics simulations. We find a more circularized disk forms about 24 days following the stream–stream collision, once the mass fallback rate passes its peak and the debris stream density decreases. Despite the absence of a circularized disk at early times, various shocks and the asymmetric photosphere are sufficient to drive a wide range of X-ray-to-optical ratios and soft X-ray variability. We find that with strong apsidal precession, the first light is from the stream–stream collision. It launches an optically thick outflow, but only produces modest prompt emission. The subsequent optical and UV light-curve rise is mainly powered by shocks in the turbulent accretion flow close to the black hole. The optical–UV luminosity peaks roughly when the disk forms and shock-driven outflows subside. Radiation pressure clears the polar region and creates optically thin channels. We obtain the broadband spectral energy distribution (SED) by postprocessing multigroup simulations with 16–20 frequency groups. The SED has a blackbody component that peaks in the extreme-UV. The soft X-ray component either resembles a thermal tail, or can be described by a power law associated with bulk Compton scattering. The blackbody parameters are broadly consistent with observed optical TDEs and vary weakly with viewing angle, but the soft X-ray emission is highly angle dependent.</p></div><div class="art-list-item-tools small wd-abstr-lower"><a class="mr-2" href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e">https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae953e</a></div></div></li></ul><!--    articleEntryList end--></div></div></div><!-- End Latest tabpanel --><!-- Express Letters tabpanel --><!-- Express Letters tabpanel --><!-- Start Review tabpanel --><!-- End Review tabpanel --><!-- Start Featured tabpanel --><!-- End Featured tabpanel --><!-- Start Editor's chocie tabpanel --><!-- End Editor's chocie tabpanel --><!-- Start AM tabpanel --><!-- End AM tabpanel --><!-- Start Trending tabpanel --><!-- End Trending tabpanel --><!-- Start Open Access tabpanel --><!-- End Open Access tabpanel --><!-- Start Spotlights tabpanel --><!-- End Spotlights tabpanel --><!-- MostCited tabpanel --><div tabindex="0"
                 role="tabpanel"
                 id="most-cited-tab"
                 aria-labelledby="most-cited" hidden="hidden"><div class="
    reveal-container reveal-closed reveal-enabled
    reveal-container--jnl-tab"><h2 class="tabpanel__title"><button type="button"
                    class="reveal-trigger event_tabs-accordion"
                    aria-expanded="false"><svg aria-hidden="true" class="fa-icon fa-icon--left fa-icon--flip" role="img" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><!--caret-down--><!--!Font Awesome Free 6.5.1 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free Copyright 2024 Fonticons, Inc.--><path d="M137.4 374.6c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128c9.2-9.2 11.9-22.9 6.9-34.9s-16.6-19.8-29.6-19.8L32 192c-12.9 0-24.6 7.8-29.6 19.8s-2.2 25.7 6.9 34.9l128 128z"/></svg>Most cited articles</button></h2><div class="reveal-content tabpanel__content" style="display: none"><p><button
